export const D6={title:'Infectious disease transmission and WHO priorities',hook:'Measles is so contagious that one infected person can infect 12 to 18 others in a fully susceptible population, while influenza typically spreads to one or two. These differences determine how fast an epidemic grows and how many people must be immune to stop it. The World Health Organization coordinates the global fight against these diseases.',objectives:['Explain modes of transmission, incubation period and the basic reproduction number.','Calculate the herd immunity threshold.','Describe the World Health Organization and the International Health Regulations.','Summarize key facts about priority infectious diseases from WHO fact sheets.'],sections:[
['Transmission and key time periods',[
'**Direct transmission** includes **direct contact** (touching, kissing, sexual contact) and **droplet spread** (large respiratory droplets over short distances). **Indirect transmission** includes **airborne** spread (tiny particles that stay suspended, as with measles and tuberculosis), **vehicles** (contaminated food, water, blood or objects—**fomites**) and **vectors** (mosquitoes, ticks and fleas). Some diseases are **zoonoses**, spread from animals to humans (rabies, avian influenza, Lyme disease).',
'The **incubation period** is the time from infection to symptoms; the **latent period** is the time from infection to becoming infectious; the **infectious period** is when a person can spread the agent. If people are infectious **before** symptoms (as with influenza and COVID-19), control is harder. A **carrier** harbors the agent without symptoms and can spread it (the classic example is “Typhoid Mary” Mallon).',
sort('Mode of transmission',['Direct','Airborne','Vehicle','Vector'],[['Measles in a waiting room',1],['Salmonella in undercooked chicken',2],['Malaria from Anopheles mosquitoes',3],['Mononucleosis from kissing',0],['Cholera in contaminated water',2],['Lyme disease from a tick',3]])]],
['The reproduction number and herd immunity',[
'The **basic reproduction number (R₀)** is the average number of secondary cases produced by one case in a **completely susceptible** population. The **effective reproduction number (Rₜ or R)** reflects current immunity and control measures. If R is **above 1**, cases increase; **equal to 1**, the disease is stable (endemic); **below 1**, the outbreak shrinks. R depends on contact rates, the probability of transmission per contact and the duration of infectiousness.',
'**Herd (community) immunity** occurs when enough people are immune that transmission chains break, protecting those who cannot be vaccinated. The **herd immunity threshold** is approximately **1 − 1/R₀**. For an R₀ of 4, the threshold is 1 − 1/4 = **75%**; for measles with an R₀ of about 12–18, it is about **92–94%**, which is why measles returns quickly when vaccination coverage falls.',
ex('Herd immunity thresholds','Find the threshold for an invented disease with R₀ = 5 and for measles with R₀ = 15.',['R₀ = 5: 1 − 1/5 = 1 − 0.20 = 0.80 = 80%','R₀ = 15: 1 − 1/15 ≈ 1 − 0.067 = 0.933 ≈ 93%'],'About 80% immunity for R₀ = 5 and about 93% for measles.')]],
['The World Health Organization',[
'The **World Health Organization (WHO)**, a specialized agency of the **United Nations**, was founded in **1948** and is headquartered in **Geneva, Switzerland**. Its decision-making body is the **World Health Assembly** of member states, which elects the **Director-General**; it works through **six regional offices**. WHO sets norms and standards, coordinates responses to emergencies, supports countries, monitors global health trends and publishes **fact sheets**, guidelines and the **International Classification of Diseases (ICD)**.',
'Major WHO-led successes include the **eradication of smallpox** (declared in **1980**) and the **Global Polio Eradication Initiative**, which has reduced wild poliovirus to a small number of countries. WHO also maintains the **Essential Medicines List** and runs programs on tuberculosis, HIV, malaria, immunization and noncommunicable diseases.',
flip('WHO facts',[['Founded','1948'],['Headquarters','Geneva, Switzerland'],['Governing body','World Health Assembly'],['Regional offices','Six'],['Smallpox eradication declared','1980'],['Disease classification','International Classification of Diseases']])]],
['The International Health Regulations',[
'The **International Health Regulations (IHR, 2005)** are a legally binding agreement among member states to **prevent, detect and respond** to the international spread of disease while avoiding unnecessary interference with travel and trade. Countries must develop **core capacities** for surveillance and response and **notify WHO** of events that may constitute a public health emergency of international concern.',
'The Director-General, advised by an **Emergency Committee**, can declare a **Public Health Emergency of International Concern (PHEIC)**. PHEICs have been declared for the 2009 **H1N1 influenza** pandemic, **polio** (2014), **Ebola** in West Africa (2014) and the Democratic Republic of the Congo (2019), **Zika** (2016), **COVID-19** (2020) and **mpox** (2022 and 2024). Amendments adopted in 2024 added a higher **pandemic emergency** alert level.',
order('IHR event response',['A country detects an unusual event','The country assesses it using IHR criteria','The country notifies WHO','WHO assesses the risk','The Emergency Committee advises the Director-General','A PHEIC may be declared with temporary recommendations'])]],
['Priority infectious diseases',[
'WHO fact sheets highlight diseases with the largest global burden. **Tuberculosis** (*Mycobacterium tuberculosis*, airborne) is among the leading infectious killers; treatment takes months, and drug-resistant TB is a major threat. **HIV** attacks CD4 cells; antiretroviral therapy controls the virus, and people with an undetectable viral load do not transmit it sexually. **Malaria** (*Plasmodium* parasites spread by female *Anopheles* mosquitoes) kills hundreds of thousands each year, mostly young children in sub-Saharan Africa.',
'**Measles** is vaccine-preventable but highly contagious. **Cholera** (*Vibrio cholerae*) spreads through contaminated water and causes severe watery diarrhea; **oral rehydration solution** saves most patients. **Dengue** (spread by *Aedes* mosquitoes) has increased dramatically worldwide. **Ebola** spreads through contact with body fluids and has a high case fatality rate. **Antimicrobial resistance** threatens progress against many infections.',
match('Disease and key fact',[['Tuberculosis','Airborne; months of treatment'],['Malaria','Plasmodium spread by Anopheles mosquitoes'],['Cholera','Watery diarrhea; oral rehydration'],['Dengue','Aedes mosquitoes'],['Measles','Herd immunity threshold about 93–95%'],['Ebola','Contact with body fluids; high fatality']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Falling vaccination rates',{a:{text:'An invented school district’s measles vaccination coverage drops from 96% to 85%. (Scenario invented for practice.) Why does this matter?',choices:[['Coverage is now below the ~93% herd immunity threshold, so outbreaks can spread','b','Correct.',true],['It does not matter as long as most children are vaccinated','b','Measles needs very high coverage.',false]]},b:{text:'A traveler brings measles into the district. What kind of epidemic curve would you expect?',choices:[['Propagated, with successive peaks about one incubation period apart','c','Correct. Person-to-person spread.',true],['A single point-source peak','c','Measles spreads person to person.',false]]},c:{text:'Health officials hold vaccination clinics and exclude unvaccinated exposed students to break transmission chains.',end:true}})]],
["Vaccines and immunity",["**Active immunity** develops when the body makes its own antibodies after infection or **vaccination** and is usually long-lasting. **Passive immunity** comes from antibodies made by someone else—from mother to baby across the placenta or in breast milk, or from **immune globulin**—and is temporary. Vaccine types include **live attenuated** (MMR, varicella), **inactivated** (polio injection, hepatitis A), **subunit, recombinant and conjugate** (hepatitis B, HPV, pneumococcal), **toxoid** (tetanus, diphtheria) and **mRNA** vaccines.","**Vaccine efficacy** is measured in trials and **vaccine effectiveness** in real-world use: efficacy = (risk in unvaccinated − risk in vaccinated) ÷ risk in unvaccinated. If an invented attack rate is 20% in the unvaccinated and 2% in the vaccinated, efficacy = (20 − 2) ÷ 20 = **90%**. Vaccine-preventable disease outbreaks occur when coverage falls below the herd immunity threshold or when immunity wanes.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Active or passive immunity?","buckets":["Active","Passive"],"items":[["Measles vaccine",0],["Antibodies from mother to newborn",1],["Recovering from chickenpox",0],["Immune globulin after hepatitis A exposure",1],["Tetanus toxoid booster",0]]}]]
],takeaways:['Transmission: direct contact and droplets; indirect by airborne particles, vehicles (food, water, fomites) and vectors; zoonoses come from animals; carriers can spread disease without symptoms.','R₀ is the average number of secondary cases in a fully susceptible population; R above 1 grows, below 1 shrinks; herd immunity threshold ≈ 1 − 1/R₀ (measles about 93%).','WHO: UN agency founded 1948 in Geneva, World Health Assembly, six regions; IHR (2005) require countries to detect and notify; PHEIC declarations include H1N1, polio, Ebola, Zika, COVID-19 and mpox.'],terms:[['Basic reproduction number (R₀)','Average secondary cases from one case in a fully susceptible population.'],['Herd immunity','Indirect protection when enough people are immune to stop transmission.'],['PHEIC','Public Health Emergency of International Concern, declared under the IHR.']],mcqs:[q('If R₀ is 4, the herd immunity threshold is about:','75%','25%','40%','96%','1 − 1/4.'),q('An epidemic is growing when R is:','Greater than 1','Equal to 1','Less than 1','Zero','Each case infects more than one.'),q('Where is WHO headquartered?','Geneva, Switzerland','Atlanta, Georgia','New York City','Paris, France','UN agency.'),q('Which agreement requires countries to notify WHO of possible international health emergencies?','International Health Regulations','Geneva Conventions','Paris Agreement','HIPAA','2005.'),q('Malaria is transmitted by:','Female Anopheles mosquitoes','Aedes mosquitoes only','Ticks','Contaminated water','Plasmodium parasites.'),q('Contaminated doorknobs that spread infection are:','Fomites','Vectors','Reservoirs','Hosts','Indirect transmission.'),q('Which disease was declared eradicated in 1980?','Smallpox','Polio','Measles','Malaria','WHO-led campaign.'),q('The time from infection to symptoms is the:','Incubation period','Infectious period','Latent period','Convalescent period','Varies by disease.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the basic reproduction number and herd immunity, calculate the herd immunity threshold for an invented disease, and describe the role of WHO and the International Health Regulations in controlling infectious diseases.',rubric:[['R₀',['average','secondary cases','susceptible','greater than 1','less than 1']],['Herd immunity',['immune','break','chains','protect','cannot be vaccinated','1 − 1/r']],['Calculation',['r₀','threshold','%','invented']],['WHO and IHR',['1948','geneva','world health assembly','international health regulations','notify','pheic','emergency committee']]],model:'The basic reproduction number, R₀, is the average number of secondary cases produced by one infectious person in a completely susceptible population; when the effective reproduction number is greater than 1 an outbreak grows, and when it falls below 1 the outbreak shrinks. R₀ depends on contact rates, the chance of transmission per contact and how long a person is infectious. Herd immunity occurs when enough of the population is immune—through vaccination or prior infection—that chains of transmission break, indirectly protecting people who cannot be vaccinated, such as newborns or people with weakened immune systems. The herd immunity threshold is about 1 − 1/R₀. For an invented disease with an R₀ of 6, the threshold is 1 − 1/6 ≈ 0.83, so about 83% of the population must be immune, while measles, with an R₀ of 12–18, requires about 93% or more. The World Health Organization, a United Nations agency founded in 1948 and headquartered in Geneva, sets global health standards, coordinates emergency responses and supports countries through programs such as polio eradication. Under the International Health Regulations of 2005, countries must build surveillance and response capacity and notify WHO of events that might spread internationally. The Director-General, advised by an Emergency Committee, can declare a Public Health Emergency of International Concern and issue temporary recommendations, as happened for Ebola, Zika, COVID-19 and mpox.'},sources:[RULES,WHO_FACTS,WHO_IHR]};

export const D7={title:'CDC and the U.S. government response to infectious diseases',hook:'When a cluster of unusual pneumonia cases appeared among young men in Los Angeles in 1981, a short CDC report described them before anyone knew the cause. It was the first published report of what became the AIDS epidemic. The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, state and local health departments and other federal agencies form the system that detects and responds to threats like this.',objectives:['Describe CDC’s history, mission and major activities.','Explain the roles of state and local health departments and other federal agencies.','Distinguish isolation and quarantine and describe legal authorities.','Describe U.S. tools for responding to outbreaks and emergencies.'],sections:[
['CDC’s history and mission',[
'CDC began in **1946** in **Atlanta, Georgia**, as the **Communicable Disease Center**, growing out of a wartime program to control malaria in the southern United States. It is part of the **U.S. Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)**. Its mission is to protect America from health, safety and security threats, both foreign and domestic, whether diseases start at home or abroad, are chronic or acute, curable or preventable, or result from human error or deliberate attack.',
'CDC conducts **surveillance**, **laboratory science**, **outbreak investigation**, **prevention research**, **health communication** and **support for state and local health departments**. It publishes the **MMWR**, runs the **Epidemic Intelligence Service (EIS)**—a two-year training program for “disease detectives” founded in **1951**—and manages programs such as **PulseNet**, **FoodNet**, the **Vaccines for Children** program and global health security work. CDC’s **Emergency Operations Center** coordinates responses to outbreaks and disasters.',
flip('CDC facts',[['Founded','1946 as the Communicable Disease Center'],['Headquarters','Atlanta, Georgia'],['Parent department','Health and Human Services'],['Disease detectives','Epidemic Intelligence Service, 1951'],['Weekly publication','MMWR'],['Foodborne DNA network','PulseNet']])]],
['The public health system: federal, state and local',[
'In the United States, **states** hold primary legal authority for public health under their **police powers**, and many delegate work to **local health departments**, which investigate cases, trace contacts, inspect restaurants, run immunization clinics and issue local orders. **CDC** provides expertise, laboratory support, funding and national coordination but usually enters a state investigation at the state’s **invitation** (an “Epi-Aid”).',
'Other federal agencies also play roles: the **Food and Drug Administration (FDA)** regulates most food, drugs, vaccines and medical devices; the **U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) Food Safety and Inspection Service** regulates meat, poultry and processed eggs; the **National Institutes of Health (NIH)** funds and conducts research; the **Administration for Strategic Preparedness and Response (ASPR)** manages the **Strategic National Stockpile** and medical countermeasure development; and the **Environmental Protection Agency (EPA)** regulates drinking water and environmental hazards.',
match('Agency and role',[['State health department','Primary legal public health authority'],['Local health department','Contact tracing and restaurant inspections'],['FDA','Regulates drugs, vaccines and most foods'],['USDA FSIS','Regulates meat and poultry'],['NIH','Biomedical research'],['ASPR','Strategic National Stockpile']])]],
['Isolation, quarantine and legal authority',[
'**Isolation** separates people who are **sick** with a contagious disease from people who are not. **Quarantine** separates and restricts the movement of people who were **exposed** but are not yet sick, to see whether they become ill. Both may be voluntary or ordered. States and localities enforce isolation and quarantine within their borders; the federal government, through CDC, has authority to prevent the entry and **interstate spread** of communicable diseases.',
'CDC operates **quarantine stations** at major U.S. ports of entry and airports, where staff respond to reports of ill travelers. Federal isolation and quarantine are authorized by executive order for a list of diseases that includes cholera, diphtheria, infectious tuberculosis, plague, smallpox, yellow fever, viral hemorrhagic fevers, severe acute respiratory syndromes and novel influenza capable of causing a pandemic. Public health orders must balance community protection with individual rights.',
sort('Isolation or quarantine?',['Isolation','Quarantine'],[['A patient with active TB stays home until no longer infectious',0],['A traveler exposed to measles stays home for the incubation period',1],['A hospitalized Ebola patient is kept in a special unit',0],['Contacts of a mumps case are monitored at home',1],['A student with confirmed flu stays out of class',0]])]],
['Responding to outbreaks and emergencies',[
'Government tools include **disease reporting laws**, **contact tracing**, **vaccination campaigns** and **post-exposure prophylaxis**, **product recalls** (FDA and USDA), **boil-water advisories**, **school or event closures**, **travel health notices** and **public communication**. The **HHS Secretary** can declare a **public health emergency**, which frees funding and flexibilities; the **FDA** can issue **Emergency Use Authorizations (EUAs)** for unapproved medical products during declared emergencies.',
'The **Strategic National Stockpile** holds medicines, vaccines and supplies for emergencies too large for local resources. **Vaccine recommendations** are developed by advisory committees and issued by CDC; state laws set school vaccination requirements. Effective response depends on **trust** and clear, consistent **risk communication**—being first, right and credible.',
order('A foodborne outbreak response',['Clinicians and labs report cases','PulseNet links cases with matching DNA','Health departments interview patients','Investigators identify a common food','FDA or USDA works with companies on a recall','CDC and partners communicate and monitor for new cases'])]],
['Global health security',[
'Infectious diseases cross borders quickly, so U.S. agencies work internationally. CDC has offices in dozens of countries, trains field epidemiologists through **Field Epidemiology Training Programs (FETP)** and supports laboratory and surveillance capacity. The **Global Health Security Agenda** helps countries prevent, detect and respond to threats under the IHR. Collaboration with WHO and other countries is essential for early warning about diseases such as novel influenza, Ebola and mpox.',
'The **One Health** approach recognizes that human, animal and environmental health are connected—about three in four new or emerging infectious diseases come from animals. Public health, veterinary and environmental agencies share data on threats such as avian influenza in poultry and dairy cattle, rabies and antimicrobial resistance.',
chk('Which approach links human, animal and environmental health?',['One Health','Hands-only CPR','Herd immunity','Passive surveillance'],'Many emerging infections are zoonotic.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Ill traveler',{a:{text:'An airline reports a passenger with fever and rash arriving from a country with a measles outbreak. (Scenario invented for practice.) Which federal unit responds at the airport?',choices:[['A CDC quarantine station','b','Correct.',true],['The USDA Food Safety and Inspection Service','b','USDA regulates meat and poultry.',false]]},b:{text:'Measles is confirmed. What happens to unvaccinated passengers seated nearby?',choices:[['Contact tracing with possible post-exposure vaccination and quarantine','c','Correct.',true],['Isolation, because they are already sick','c','Exposed but not sick means quarantine.',false]]},c:{text:'State and local health departments monitor contacts in their jurisdictions and report to CDC.',end:true}})]],
["Communicating risk during outbreaks",["During an emergency, people need clear, accurate information to protect themselves. CDC’s **Crisis and Emergency Risk Communication (CERC)** principles are **be first**, **be right**, **be credible**, **express empathy**, **promote action** and **show respect**. Messages should say what is known, what is not yet known, what is being done and what people should do—and be updated as information changes.","Misinformation spreads quickly on social media. Public health agencies counter it by communicating early and often, using trusted messengers such as local clinicians and community leaders, translating materials into multiple languages and explaining the reasons behind recommendations. Trust built before a crisis makes response more effective.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"CERC principle and example","pairs":[["Be first","Announce the outbreak quickly"],["Be right","Share accurate, verified facts"],["Be credible","Be honest about what is unknown"],["Express empathy","Acknowledge fear and suffering"],["Promote action","Tell people what to do"],["Show respect","Treat all communities with dignity"]]}]]
],takeaways:['CDC: founded 1946 in Atlanta as the Communicable Disease Center, part of HHS; conducts surveillance, labs, investigations and prevention; publishes MMWR; EIS disease detectives since 1951.','States hold primary public health authority (police powers) with local health departments; FDA regulates drugs, vaccines and most food; USDA FSIS meat and poultry; NIH research; ASPR manages the Strategic National Stockpile.','Isolation separates the sick; quarantine separates the exposed; CDC quarantine stations at ports of entry; tools include reporting, contact tracing, vaccination, recalls, emergency declarations and EUAs; One Health links human, animal and environmental health.'],terms:[['Quarantine','Separating people exposed to a contagious disease who are not yet sick.'],['Isolation','Separating sick people with a contagious disease from healthy people.'],['Epidemic Intelligence Service','CDC’s training program for field epidemiologists.']],mcqs:[q('CDC was founded in 1946 as the:','Communicable Disease Center','Center for Drug Control','Clinical Data Center','Council for Disease Care','Atlanta, Georgia.'),q('Separating exposed people who are not yet sick is:','Quarantine','Isolation','Surveillance','Triage','Watch for illness.'),q('Which agency regulates vaccines and most foods?','FDA','USDA FSIS','EPA','NIH','Food and Drug Administration.'),q('Who holds primary legal authority for public health in the United States?','States','CDC','WHO','Hospitals','Police powers.'),q('CDC’s two-year training program for disease detectives is the:','Epidemic Intelligence Service','Peace Corps','Medical Reserve Corps','PulseNet','Founded 1951.'),q('The Strategic National Stockpile is managed by:','ASPR','USDA','EPA','FDA only','Medical countermeasures.'),q('Where does CDC operate quarantine stations?','At major ports of entry and airports','In every hospital','In schools','In WHO offices','Ill travelers.'),q('The idea that human, animal and environmental health are linked is:','One Health','Herd immunity','Primary prevention','Point source','Zoonoses.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how federal, state and local agencies work together to respond to a multistate foodborne outbreak, and explain the difference between isolation and quarantine.',rubric:[['Detection',['report','laboratory','pulsenet','dna','whole genome','link','surveillance']],['State and local roles',['state','local','interview','authority','police powers','inspection']],['Federal roles',['cdc','coordinate','fda','usda','recall','communicate','mmwr']],['Isolation and quarantine',['isolation','sick','quarantine','exposed','not yet sick','stations','interstate']]],model:'A multistate foodborne outbreak usually begins when clinicians and laboratories report infections, such as Salmonella, to local and state health departments, as required by state reporting laws. State public health laboratories sequence the bacteria and upload the DNA fingerprints to CDC’s PulseNet, which links cases with matching patterns across states. Local and state health departments, which hold primary public health authority, interview patients about what they ate, inspect restaurants or stores and collect food samples. CDC coordinates the multistate investigation, analyzes the combined data, compares what ill people ate with what healthy people typically eat and communicates with the public, often publishing the findings in the MMWR. Once a common food is identified, the FDA—or USDA’s Food Safety and Inspection Service for meat, poultry and processed eggs—traces the product back through the supply chain and works with companies on recalls, while surveillance continues until no new cases appear. For contagious diseases, isolation and quarantine are also tools: isolation separates people who are sick with a contagious disease from those who are well, while quarantine separates and restricts the movement of people who were exposed but are not yet sick to see whether they develop illness. States enforce both within their borders, and CDC has federal authority to prevent the entry and interstate spread of listed diseases, operating quarantine stations at major ports of entry.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_ABOUT,CDC_EIS,CDC_QUAR,PULSENET]};

export const D8={title:'Reading the MMWR',hook:'Half of the Epidemiology test comes from CDC’s Morbidity and Mortality Weekly Report. Each report is short, but it packs in a public health question, data, a conclusion and recommendations. Learning the structure of an MMWR report lets you read any issue quickly and pick out exactly what test questions ask about.',objectives:['Describe the MMWR and its publication series.','Use the summary box to identify a report’s key findings.','Interpret tables, figures, rates and confidence intervals in a report.','Distinguish findings, interpretations and limitations.'],sections:[
['What the MMWR is',[
'The **Morbidity and Mortality Weekly Report (MMWR)** is CDC’s primary vehicle for scientific publication of **timely, reliable, authoritative, accurate, objective and useful** public health information and recommendations. It has been called “the voice of CDC.” Its readers include physicians, nurses, epidemiologists, public health workers, researchers, educators and the media. Reports are written by CDC staff and state and local health departments.',
'The MMWR series includes the **MMWR Weekly** (full reports, **Notes from the Field** and **QuickStats** graphs), **Surveillance Summaries** (detailed analyses of surveillance data, such as the Youth Risk Behavior Survey results), **Recommendations and Reports** (official guidelines, such as vaccine or treatment recommendations) and **Supplements**. Historic reports include the first description of what became AIDS in **June 1981**.',
match('MMWR series and content',[['MMWR Weekly','Timely full reports and notes'],['Notes from the Field','Brief reports of new events'],['QuickStats','A single graph of national data'],['Surveillance Summaries','In-depth analysis of surveillance data'],['Recommendations and Reports','Official guidelines'],['June 1981 report','First description of what became AIDS']])]],
['The structure of a full report',[
'Most full reports follow a predictable structure. A short **introduction** explains the problem and why it matters. **Methods** describe the data source, population, case definition, time period and analyses. **Results** present the numbers—often with tables and figures. The **Discussion** interprets the findings, compares them with previous data and lists **limitations**. The report ends with **implications for public health practice** and often recommendations.',
'Every full report has a **summary box** with three questions: **What is already known about this topic? What is added by this report? What are the implications for public health practice?** Read this box first—it states the report’s core message in a few sentences and is a common source of test questions.',
order('Reading an MMWR report efficiently',['Read the title and summary box','Note the population, place and time period','Identify the data source and case definition','Find the key numbers in results, tables and figures','Read the discussion and limitations','Write the implications in your own words'])]],
['Interpreting numbers in reports',[
'Reports use counts, **percentages**, **rates** (per 100,000 population), **rate ratios**, **odds ratios** and **trends** (such as annual percent change). Note the **denominator** and the **time period** for each number. A **95% confidence interval (CI)** shows the range of values compatible with the data; a narrow interval means more precision. If the CI for a ratio includes **1**, or the CI for a difference includes **0**, the result is not statistically significant at the usual level.',
ex('Reading a confidence interval','An invented report states: “Adolescents who used e-cigarettes had higher odds of reporting persistent sadness (OR = 1.8; 95% CI = 1.5–2.2).”',['The OR of 1.8 means 80% higher odds among users than non-users','The CI (1.5–2.2) does not include 1','The association is statistically significant','The study design determines whether cause can be inferred—cross-sectional surveys cannot show which came first'],'A significant association, but not proof that one caused the other (invented example).'),
'Also note whether results are **crude** or **age-adjusted**, whether data are **provisional** (subject to change) and whether numbers come from a **sample** (with weighting) or from **complete counts**.']],
['Findings, interpretations and limitations',[
'Separate what the data **show** (findings) from what the authors **conclude** (interpretation) and what might be **wrong** (limitations). Typical limitations include **underreporting** or incomplete surveillance, **self-reported** data subject to recall or social desirability bias, **cross-sectional** designs that cannot establish temporality, results that may not be **generalizable** beyond the population studied, **small numbers** and changes in testing or case definitions over time.',
'Test questions often ask about the population studied, the time period, the main finding, a specific number, the recommended action or a limitation. Read the actual reports listed on CDC’s MMWR site, and practice summarizing each in three sentences: what was studied, what was found and what should be done.',
bench('Is the claim supported by this invented report excerpt?',[{text:'Invented excerpt: In 2025, among 4,200 surveyed high school students in one state, 31% reported fewer than 7 hours of sleep on school nights. Students reporting less sleep were more likely to report poor grades.',claim:'About 31% of the surveyed students reported fewer than 7 hours of sleep on school nights.',supported:true,why:'The excerpt states this percentage directly.'},{text:'Invented excerpt: In 2025, among 4,200 surveyed high school students in one state, 31% reported fewer than 7 hours of sleep on school nights. Students reporting less sleep were more likely to report poor grades.',claim:'Short sleep caused the students’ poor grades.',supported:false,why:'A cross-sectional survey shows an association, not which came first or causation.'},{text:'Invented excerpt: Reported cases of an invented infection rose from 120 in 2023 to 180 in 2025 after a new, more sensitive laboratory test was introduced in 2024.',claim:'The increase in reported cases may partly reflect improved testing.',supported:true,why:'A change in testing is a recognized limitation that can increase reported cases.'},{text:'Invented excerpt: Vaccination coverage among kindergartners in an invented state was 91% in 2025, below the 95% target.',claim:'Every kindergarten in the state had coverage below 95%.',supported:false,why:'A statewide average does not describe every school.'}])]],
['Common MMWR topics',[
'MMWR reports cover the full range of public health. **Infectious diseases**: measles importations, foodborne outbreaks, influenza and respiratory virus seasons, tuberculosis, sexually transmitted infections, vaccine coverage and vector-borne diseases. **Chronic diseases**: diabetes, heart disease, cancer screening and obesity. **Injury and violence**: drug overdose deaths, suicide, motor vehicle crashes and firearm injuries. **Environmental and occupational health**: carbon monoxide poisoning, heat-related illness, lead exposure and workplace hazards. **Maternal and child health**: infant mortality, birth defects and adolescent behaviors.',
'For each report you read, connect it to the concepts in this course: What **measure** is used (incidence, prevalence, rate ratio)? What **study design** or **surveillance system** produced the data? What **level of prevention** do the recommendations address?',
sort('Topic area',['Infectious disease','Chronic disease','Injury and violence','Environmental health'],[['Measles cases linked to international travel',0],['Diabetes prevalence by state',1],['Drug overdose deaths by county',2],['Carbon monoxide poisonings after a storm',3],['Kindergarten vaccination coverage',0],['Heat-related emergency department visits',3]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Reading a summary box',{a:{text:'An invented summary box says: “What is added by this report? During 2025, emergency department visits for heat illness increased 25% compared with 2023, with the highest rates among adults aged 18–44 years.” (Invented for practice.) What is the main finding?',choices:[['Heat-illness ED visits rose 25% from 2023, highest in adults 18–44','b','Correct.',true],['Heat illness is now the leading cause of death','b','The box does not say that.',false]]},b:{text:'Which surveillance approach most likely produced these data?',choices:[['Syndromic surveillance of emergency department visits','c','Correct.',true],['A randomized controlled trial','c','No intervention was assigned.',false]]},c:{text:'You note the implication: heat action plans and outreach to young adult workers.',end:true}})]],
["Building an MMWR study routine",["Because the MMWR makes up half of the test, build a routine. Each week, read the **summary boxes** of the new reports and create a **one-page note** for each: title, date, population, data source, design, key numbers, main finding, limitation and recommendation. Group notes by topic so that you can compare related reports—for example, all reports about measles or all reports about overdose.","Quiz yourself with questions like those on the test: “Which population had the highest rate?” “What surveillance system provided the data?” “What did the authors recommend?” Practice calculations from reported numbers, such as percentage changes and rate ratios. Review **QuickStats** graphs by stating the main pattern in one sentence. Spaced review over several months works better than cramming the week before.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"One-page MMWR note","steps":["Title and publication date","Population, place and time","Data source and design","Key numbers","Main finding","Limitation","Recommendation"]}]]
],takeaways:['MMWR is CDC’s primary vehicle for timely public health information; series include Weekly reports (with Notes from the Field and QuickStats), Surveillance Summaries, Recommendations and Reports and Supplements; first AIDS report June 1981.','Full reports have an introduction, methods, results, discussion with limitations and implications, plus a summary box: What is already known? What is added? What are the implications for public health practice?','Note the population, time, denominator and measure; a 95% CI for a ratio that excludes 1 is significant; separate findings from interpretation and limitations (underreporting, self-report, cross-sectional design, generalizability, testing changes).'],terms:[['MMWR','CDC’s Morbidity and Mortality Weekly Report.'],['Summary box','The three-question box that states a report’s key message.'],['Confidence interval','A range of values compatible with the data, often 95%.']],mcqs:[q('Which MMWR series publishes official guidelines?','Recommendations and Reports','QuickStats','Notes from the Field','Supplements only','Such as vaccine recommendations.'),q('Which question appears in the MMWR summary box?','What is added by this report?','Who funded the study?','What is the author’s favorite finding?','How long did it take?','Plus what is already known and implications.'),q('A 95% CI for an odds ratio of 1.2 to 2.4 means the association is:','Statistically significant','Not significant','Protective','Impossible to interpret','The CI excludes 1.'),q('A 95% CI for a risk ratio of 0.8 to 1.6 means:','The result is not statistically significant','A strong harmful effect','A strong protective effect','The study is invalid','It includes 1.'),q('Self-reported survey data are prone to:','Recall and social desirability bias','Randomization','No bias','Loss to follow-up only','Common limitation.'),q('A single graph of national data in the MMWR is called:','QuickStats','Surveillance Summary','Notes from the Field','Recommendation','Short graphic feature.'),q('The first MMWR report describing what became AIDS was published in:','1981','1961','1991','2001','June 5, 1981.'),q('Which should you read first in an MMWR report?','The summary box','The references','The author list','The acknowledgments','Core message.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to read an MMWR report efficiently and how to evaluate its findings, including the summary box, key numbers and limitations.',rubric:[['Structure',['title','summary box','already known','added','implications','methods','results','discussion']],['Numbers',['population','time','denominator','rate','percentage','ratio','confidence interval','1']],['Evaluation',['finding','interpretation','limitations','underreporting','self-report','cross-sectional','generalizable']],['Application',['design','surveillance','measure','prevention','recommendation','summarize']]],model:'I start with the title and the summary box, which answers three questions: what is already known about the topic, what is added by this report and what the implications are for public health practice. That gives me the core message before I read the details. Next, I identify the population, place and time period, the data source—such as a surveillance system or survey—and the case definition used. In the results, tables and figures, I find the key numbers and note whether each is a count, percentage, rate per 100,000, rate ratio or odds ratio, what the denominator is and whether rates are age-adjusted or provisional. I check confidence intervals: if a 95% interval for a ratio excludes 1, the association is statistically significant. In the discussion, I separate the findings, which are what the data show, from the authors’ interpretation and from the limitations, such as underreporting, self-reported data subject to recall bias, cross-sectional designs that cannot establish which came first, results that may not generalize beyond the population studied or changes in testing that alter case counts. Finally, I connect the report to epidemiology concepts—the measure, the study design or surveillance system and the level of prevention its recommendations target—and summarize it in three sentences: what was studied, what was found and what should be done.'},sources:[RULES,MMWR,CDC_ABOUT]};

export const D9={title:'MMWR outbreak investigations and case studies',hook:'Many MMWR reports tell the story of an investigation: a cluster is noticed, cases are counted, hypotheses are tested and a source is found. Reading these reports as detective stories helps you remember them and answer questions about how investigators reached their conclusions. This lesson walks through invented MMWR-style cases built on real investigation methods.',objectives:['Apply outbreak investigation steps to MMWR-style reports.','Calculate and compare food-specific attack rates.','Interpret laboratory evidence such as whole genome sequencing.','Identify control measures and lessons for public health practice.'],sections:[
['How investigation reports are organized',[
'An outbreak report usually begins with how the problem was **detected**—a clinician’s call, a laboratory match in PulseNet, a spike in syndromic data or a school absence report. It then describes the **case definition**, **case finding** (how many confirmed and probable cases), the **descriptive epidemiology** (ages, sex, onset dates, locations), the **epidemic curve**, the **analytic study** and **laboratory** and **environmental** findings. It ends with **control measures** and implications.',
'When you read one, ask: How was it detected? What was the case definition? What design tested the hypothesis? What was the measure of association? What evidence linked the source—epidemiologic, laboratory or traceback? What was done to stop it?',
order('Typical outbreak report',['Detection of the problem','Case definition and case finding','Descriptive epidemiology and epidemic curve','Analytic study','Laboratory and environmental findings','Control measures and implications'])]],
['Food-specific attack rates',[
'In a **retrospective cohort** of everyone at an event, investigators calculate the **attack rate** for people who ate each food and for people who did not, then compare them. The likely vehicle has a **high attack rate among those who ate it**, a **low attack rate among those who did not**, the **largest risk ratio** and accounts for **most cases**.',
"Also check for a **dose–response** pattern (people who ate more of the food were more likely to become ill) and for **cases among non-eaters**, which may reflect cross-contamination, secondary spread or people who forgot what they ate. A food eaten by almost everyone can have a high attack rate among eaters but a small risk ratio, because there are too few non-eaters for comparison.",
{t:'table',caption:'Invented banquet outbreak: food-specific attack rates',head:['Food','Ate: ill/total','Attack rate','Did not eat: ill/total','Attack rate','Risk ratio'],rows:[['Chicken','30/60','50%','10/40','25%','2.0'],['Potato salad','36/45','80%','4/55','7%','11.0'],['Rolls','32/80','40%','8/20','40%','1.0'],['Fruit cup','20/50','40%','20/50','40%','1.0']]},
ex('Finding the likely vehicle','Use the invented table to identify the most likely vehicle.',['Potato salad attack rate among eaters = 36 ÷ 45 = 80%','Among non-eaters = 4 ÷ 55 ≈ 7%','Risk ratio ≈ 0.80 ÷ 0.073 ≈ 11','Potato salad explains 36 of 40 cases (90%)','Rolls and fruit cup have a risk ratio of 1.0—no association'],'Potato salad is the most likely vehicle (data invented for practice).')]],
['Laboratory evidence and traceback',[
'**Laboratory confirmation** identifies the pathogen in patients and, ideally, in the suspected food or environment. **Whole genome sequencing (WGS)** compares the DNA of bacteria from patients and food; isolates that are **closely related genetically** support a common source. PulseNet uses WGS to link illnesses across states, sometimes before anyone realizes an outbreak exists. **Serotyping** and **antimicrobial resistance testing** add information.',
'**Traceback** follows the suspected food from the point of sale back through distributors to the farm or processor; **traceforward** identifies other places that received the same lot. Strong conclusions combine **epidemiologic**, **laboratory** and **traceback** evidence. Sometimes the source is never confirmed, and the report says so honestly.',
"Reports also describe **why** contamination happened—for example, irrigation water contaminated by nearby cattle operations, an ill food worker, inadequate cooking or holding food at unsafe temperatures. These root causes guide long-term prevention, such as produce safety rules, worker sick-leave policies and food-handler training.",
match('Evidence type and example',[['Epidemiologic','Ill people ate the same food more often'],['Laboratory','Same bacterial strain in patients and food'],['Whole genome sequencing','Isolates closely related genetically'],['Traceback','Supply chain followed to one farm'],['Environmental','Sampling a kitchen or water system'],['Traceforward','Finding other stores that received the lot']])]],
['Investigating person-to-person and environmental outbreaks',[
'Not every outbreak is foodborne. **Measles** reports often describe an **importation** by an unvaccinated traveler, spread in communities with low coverage, contact tracing, post-exposure vaccination or immune globulin and exclusion of susceptible contacts for **21 days**. **Legionnaires’ disease** reports trace cases to building water systems or cooling towers. **Norovirus** outbreaks on cruise ships and in long-term care facilities spread person to person and through contaminated surfaces.',
'**Environmental and toxic** investigations include carbon monoxide poisoning after storms (generators used indoors), lead poisoning from imported products and illnesses from contaminated recreational water. In each case, the same steps apply: define cases, find them, describe them, test hypotheses and act.',
sort('Likely transmission pattern',['Common source','Person-to-person'],[['Legionnaires’ disease from a cooling tower',0],['Measles spreading in an undervaccinated community',1],['Salmonella from contaminated sprouts',0],['Norovirus spreading among nursing home residents',1],['Carbon monoxide from a generator in a garage',0]])]],
['Control measures and lessons learned',[
'Reports describe **control measures** matched to the source: **recalls** and public warnings for food, **disinfection** and water system remediation for Legionella, **exclusion of ill food workers** for norovirus and hepatitis A, **vaccination clinics** for measles or hepatitis A, **education** on generator safety for carbon monoxide and **policy changes** such as new food safety rules.',
'The **“implications for public health practice”** section often recommends actions for clinicians (report suspected cases, test, vaccinate), for health departments (improve surveillance, coordinate) and for the public (cook foods thoroughly, stay home when sick). Memorize the main recommendation of each report you study.',
match('Source and control measure',[['Contaminated produce','Recall and public warning'],['Cooling tower','Disinfect and remediate the water system'],['Ill food worker with hepatitis A','Exclude from work; vaccinate contacts'],['Measles importation','Contact tracing and vaccination'],['Generator indoors','Education on carbon monoxide safety'],['Cruise ship norovirus','Enhanced cleaning and isolation of ill passengers']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Multistate Salmonella cluster',{a:{text:'PulseNet identifies 48 Salmonella cases in 9 states with closely related WGS patterns. Interviews show 40 of 44 interviewed patients ate fresh basil the week before illness, compared with about 15% of healthy people in a population survey. (Scenario invented for practice.) What does this suggest?',choices:[['Basil is a likely vehicle because exposure among cases is far higher than expected','b','Correct.',true],['The cases are unrelated','b','WGS and exposure data link them.',false]]},b:{text:'What additional evidence would most strengthen the conclusion?',choices:[['Isolating the same strain from basil and tracing it to a common supplier','c','Correct. Laboratory and traceback evidence.',true],['Counting more cases in one state','c','More counts do not identify the source.',false]]},c:{text:'FDA traceback identifies one grower; a recall is issued, and CDC continues surveillance until new cases stop.',end:true}})]],
["Health care–associated and occupational investigations",["Many MMWR reports describe outbreaks in **health care settings**: infections linked to contaminated medications or devices, *Candida auris* spreading between patients, hepatitis C transmitted through unsafe injection practices and Legionella in hospital water systems. Investigators review medical records, observe infection control practices, test environmental samples and compare cases with controls to find the source. Control measures include **contact precautions**, **cleaning and disinfection**, **screening patients** and **removing contaminated products**.","**Occupational** investigations examine illness among workers—silicosis among engineered-stone countertop fabricators, heat illness among farm workers, lead poisoning among construction workers and infections among animal handlers. These reports identify the **exposure**, the **workplace practices** that allowed it and **engineering controls**, protective equipment and policies that prevent future cases. NIOSH often partners in these investigations.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Setting and typical investigation finding","pairs":[["Hospital ICU","Spread of a drug-resistant organism between patients"],["Outpatient clinic","Unsafe injection practices"],["Hospital water system","Legionella growth"],["Countertop fabrication shop","Silica dust exposure"],["Farm in summer","Heat-related illness"],["Bridge repair site","Lead exposure"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Outbreak reports describe detection, case definition and case finding, descriptive epidemiology and the epidemic curve, analytic study, laboratory and environmental findings, control measures and implications.','The likely vehicle has a high attack rate among eaters, a low rate among non-eaters, the largest risk ratio and explains most cases; RR of 1.0 means no association.','Strong evidence combines epidemiologic, laboratory (whole genome sequencing via PulseNet) and traceback findings; control measures include recalls, remediation, exclusion of ill workers, vaccination and education.'],terms:[['Attack rate','The proportion of exposed people who become ill during an outbreak.'],['Whole genome sequencing','Comparing the complete DNA of isolates to see if they are closely related.'],['Traceback','Following a suspected product back through the supply chain.']],mcqs:[q('In an invented outbreak, 36 of 45 people who ate potato salad became ill. What is the attack rate?','80%','36%','45%','20%','36 ÷ 45.'),q('A food with a risk ratio of 1.0 is:','Not associated with illness','The likely vehicle','Protective','Contaminated','Equal attack rates.'),q('Which laboratory method compares the complete DNA of bacterial isolates?','Whole genome sequencing','Gram stain only','Blood typing','Urinalysis','Used by PulseNet.'),q('Following a suspected food back to the farm is called:','Traceback','Traceforward','Surveillance','Contact tracing','Supply chain.'),q('Legionnaires’ disease outbreaks are often linked to:','Building water systems or cooling towers','Undercooked chicken','Mosquitoes','Person-to-person kissing','Aerosolized water.'),q('Exposed, unvaccinated contacts of measles are typically excluded for:','21 days','2 days','6 months','1 hour','Incubation period.'),q('Which design is used when investigators interview everyone who attended a banquet?','Retrospective cohort','Randomized trial','Ecological study','Case report','Compare attack rates.'),q('Strong conclusions about an outbreak source combine:','Epidemiologic, laboratory and traceback evidence','Only one interview','Only the epidemic curve','Media reports','Multiple lines of evidence.')],frq:{prompt:'Using an invented food-specific attack rate table of your own, explain how investigators identify the vehicle of a foodborne outbreak and what additional evidence and control measures they use.',rubric:[['Attack rates',['ate','did not eat','attack rate','ill','total','%']],['Comparison',['risk ratio','highest','lowest','explains','most cases','1.0']],['Additional evidence',['laboratory','whole genome','pulsenet','traceback','environmental','strain']],['Control',['recall','warning','exclude','food worker','surveillance','communicate','mmwr']]],model:'Investigators interview everyone who attended the event—a retrospective cohort—and for each food calculate the attack rate among people who ate it and among those who did not. In my invented example from a picnic, 25 of 30 people who ate the egg salad became ill (83%) compared with 3 of 50 who did not eat it (6%), giving a risk ratio of about 14; for lemonade, 15 of 50 drinkers became ill (30%) compared with 13 of 30 non-drinkers (43%), a risk ratio below 1; and for the burgers, 20 of 60 eaters became ill (33%) compared with 8 of 20 non-eaters (40%), also showing no association. The egg salad is the likely vehicle because it has a high attack rate among eaters, a low attack rate among non-eaters, the largest risk ratio and explains most cases—25 of 28. Investigators then seek laboratory evidence by testing patients’ stool and leftover egg salad and comparing isolates with whole genome sequencing through PulseNet, along with environmental evidence from the kitchen, such as improper refrigeration or an ill food worker. For commercial products they trace the food back through the supply chain. Control measures include discarding the remaining food, correcting food-handling practices, excluding ill workers, issuing recalls or public warnings when products are involved and maintaining surveillance until new cases stop, and the findings are communicated in reports such as the MMWR.'},sources:[RULES,MMWR,PULSENET]};

export const D10={title:'Surveillance summaries, recommendations and the tiebreaker essay',hook:'Some MMWR issues are not about a single outbreak but about the health of the whole nation: how many teenagers vape, how many adults have high blood pressure, how vaccine schedules should change. These surveillance summaries and recommendations shape policy for years. This lesson shows how to read them and how to write a strong essay if your score is tied.',objectives:['Describe major U.S. surveillance surveys reported in MMWR Surveillance Summaries.','Interpret prevalence estimates, trends and disparities.','Explain how evidence becomes recommendations.','Plan and write a structured epidemiology essay.'],sections:[
['Major surveillance surveys',[
'The **Youth Risk Behavior Surveillance System (YRBSS)** surveys high school students—usually every **two years**—about behaviors that contribute to the leading causes of death and disability: injury and violence, tobacco and substance use, sexual behaviors, diet, physical activity and mental health. The **Behavioral Risk Factor Surveillance System (BRFSS)** is a state-based **telephone survey** of adults about health behaviors, chronic conditions and preventive services. **NHANES** combines interviews with **physical examinations** and **laboratory tests**.',
'Other systems include the **National Vital Statistics System** (births, deaths and causes of death), the **National Notifiable Diseases Surveillance System**, the **National Violent Death Reporting System**, the **Pregnancy Risk Assessment Monitoring System (PRAMS)** and the **National Immunization Surveys**. Surveillance Summaries report **prevalence** estimates with confidence intervals, **trends** over time and **differences** among groups.',
match('System and population',[['YRBSS','High school students'],['BRFSS','Adults, by telephone'],['NHANES','Interviews, exams and lab tests'],['NVSS','Births and deaths'],['PRAMS','Women who recently gave birth'],['National Immunization Surveys','Vaccination coverage']])]],
['Interpreting prevalence, trends and disparities',[
'Surveillance Summaries describe **prevalence** (for example, the percentage of students who currently use e-cigarettes), **trends** (whether prevalence increased, decreased or stayed the same), and **differences** by sex, grade, race and ethnicity, sexual identity, region and other factors. Because surveys sample people, estimates have **confidence intervals**; overlapping intervals suggest differences may not be significant, while formal tests determine significance.',
'When describing differences, use precise language—“prevalence was higher among…”—and avoid implying that a characteristic itself causes the outcome. Differences often reflect **social determinants** such as access to care, income, discrimination and neighborhood conditions. Surveys rely on **self-report** and may not represent people who were not reached (such as students absent from school or adults without phones).',
{t:'w',kind:'equitylab',title:'Explore rate differences with invented survey data'}]],
['From evidence to recommendations',[
'**Recommendations and Reports** translate evidence into guidance—for example, immunization schedules, testing and treatment guidelines for sexually transmitted infections or tuberculosis, and infection control practices. Recommendations are developed by expert workgroups and advisory committees that review the evidence, often using a structured framework that rates **certainty of evidence** and weighs **benefits and harms**, values, acceptability, feasibility and resource use.',
'Good recommendations specify **who** they apply to, **what** action to take and **when**. They are updated as evidence changes, which is why it is important to read the most recent version. In the MMWR, recommendation reports often include a summary of changes from the previous guidance.',
"Recommendations become action through **implementation**: clinicians change practice, health departments fund programs, schools and employers adopt policies and laws set requirements. Surveillance then measures whether the recommendation is working—for example, whether vaccination coverage rises or disease rates fall after new guidance.",
order('From data to guidance',['Surveillance detects a problem or trend','Studies test causes and interventions','Experts review the body of evidence','Benefits, harms and feasibility are weighed','Recommendations are published','Implementation is monitored through surveillance'])]],
['Applying concepts to surveillance data',[
ex('Interpreting an invented surveillance summary','In an invented survey, current e-cigarette use among high school students was 20% in 2021 and 14% in 2025 (95% CIs 18–22% and 12–16%).',['Absolute change = 14% − 20% = −6 percentage points','Relative change = −6 ÷ 20 = −30%','The confidence intervals do not overlap (18–22 vs 12–16)','The decline is likely statistically significant','Self-report and survey changes are possible limitations'],'Current use fell by 6 percentage points (a 30% relative decline) in this invented example.'),
'Distinguish **absolute** change (percentage points) from **relative** change (percent). A drop from 20% to 14% is a **6 percentage point** decrease and a **30% relative** decrease. Both are correct; questions may ask for either.',
"Also distinguish **statistical significance** from **public health importance**. A very large survey can find a statistically significant difference that is small in practical terms, while a small change in a common, serious condition can affect many thousands of people. When you describe results, report the size of the change, its precision and how many people it affects."]],
['Writing the tiebreaker essay',[
'The Epidemiology essay is judged only to **break ties**, but in close competitions it can decide placement. Plan before you write: read the prompt twice, underline what it asks (define, compare, explain, apply), and outline three main points. A strong structure is an **introduction** that answers the question directly, **body paragraphs** that each make one point with specific evidence (definitions, numbers, examples from MMWR or history) and a **conclusion** that states the public health implication.',
'Use correct terms—incidence, prevalence, risk ratio, surveillance, confounding—and show reasoning, not just facts. When you use numbers, say what they mean. Avoid overstating: write “was associated with” rather than “caused” unless the evidence supports causation. Leave a few minutes to check spelling and that every part of the prompt is answered.',
order('Essay plan',['Read the prompt twice and underline the task','Outline three main points','Write an introduction that answers the question','Support each point with specific evidence','Explain the public health implication','Proofread and check every part is answered'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Interpreting a survey trend',{a:{text:'An invented summary reports that the percentage of adults with diagnosed hypertension rose from 30% to 33% over 10 years, while awareness of the condition also increased. (Scenario invented for practice.) What alternative explanation should you consider?',choices:[['More screening and diagnosis could raise the measured prevalence','b','Correct. Detection changes can affect trends.',true],['Blood pressure measurement became impossible','b','That is not supported.',false]]},b:{text:'The rise is larger among adults without health insurance. How should you describe it?',choices:[['Prevalence increased more among uninsured adults, possibly reflecting access and social factors','c','Correct. Describe without blaming.',true],['Uninsured people cause their own hypertension','c','Avoid causal and stigmatizing language.',false]]},c:{text:'You recommend expanded access to screening and treatment and continued surveillance.',end:true}})]],
["Chronic disease and injury surveillance",["Chronic diseases and injuries cause most deaths in the United States. MMWR reports track **heart disease**, **cancer**, **diabetes**, **chronic kidney disease**, **obesity** and **arthritis**, often using BRFSS, NHANES and vital statistics. Injury reports cover **drug overdose deaths** (from the National Vital Statistics System and the State Unintentional Drug Overdose Reporting System), **suicide** and **firearm injuries**, **motor vehicle crashes** and **falls** among older adults.","These reports often present **age-adjusted death rates**, trends using **annual percent change**, and differences by state, county, urbanization and demographic group. They recommend interventions at different levels of prevention: **primary** (tobacco control, naloxone distribution, safe storage), **secondary** (screening for hypertension or cancer) and **tertiary** (cardiac rehabilitation, overdose follow-up programs).",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Level of prevention in a recommendation","buckets":["Primary","Secondary","Tertiary"],"items":[["Raise the minimum age for tobacco sales",0],["Screen adults for high blood pressure",1],["Cardiac rehabilitation after a heart attack",2],["Naloxone distribution to prevent overdose deaths",0],["Colorectal cancer screening",1],["Diabetes self-management education to prevent complications",2]]}]]
],takeaways:['YRBSS surveys high school students about every two years; BRFSS surveys adults by phone; NHANES adds exams and lab tests; NVSS counts births and deaths; Surveillance Summaries report prevalence, trends and group differences with CIs.','Describe differences precisely and without implying the characteristic causes the outcome; consider social determinants and limitations such as self-report and nonresponse; recommendations weigh evidence, benefits, harms and feasibility.','Absolute change is in percentage points, relative change in percent; tiebreaker essay: answer directly, three supported points with correct terms, avoid overstating causation, state implications and proofread.'],terms:[['Surveillance Summary','An MMWR report analyzing surveillance data in depth.'],['Percentage point','The absolute difference between two percentages.'],['Recommendation','Official guidance on who should do what, based on evidence.']],mcqs:[q('Which system surveys high school students about health risk behaviors?','YRBSS','BRFSS','NHANES','VAERS','Usually every two years.'),q('Which survey includes physical exams and laboratory tests?','NHANES','BRFSS','YRBSS','NVSS','Examination survey.'),q('BRFSS collects data from adults primarily by:','Telephone survey','Physical exams','Death certificates','Hospital records only','State-based.'),q('A decline from 20% to 15% is a decrease of:','5 percentage points','5%','25 percentage points','15%','Absolute change.'),q('A decline from 20% to 15% is a relative decrease of:','25%','5%','15%','75%','5 ÷ 20.'),q('Which phrase is most appropriate for a cross-sectional finding?','Was associated with','Caused','Proved','Guaranteed','Avoid overstating.'),q('The Epidemiology essay is used to:','Break ties','Determine 50% of the score','Replace the test','Choose finalists','Tiebreaker.'),q('Which system records births and deaths?','National Vital Statistics System','YRBSS','PulseNet','VAERS','Vital statistics.')],frq:{prompt:'Choose an invented surveillance finding (such as a change in youth vaping) and explain how you would interpret the prevalence estimate, trend and group differences, including limitations and public health implications.',rubric:[['Data source',['yrbss','brfss','nhanes','survey','sample','self-report']],['Interpretation',['prevalence','percentage points','relative','confidence interval','trend','significant']],['Differences and limitations',['higher among','social determinants','access','self-report','nonresponse','causation','cross-sectional']],['Implications',['recommendation','prevention','program','policy','surveillance','continue']]],model:'In my invented example, a Youth Risk Behavior Survey found that current e-cigarette use among high school students fell from 24% in 2021 (95% CI 22–26%) to 18% in 2025 (95% CI 16–20%). This is a 6 percentage point absolute decline and a 25% relative decline, and because the confidence intervals do not overlap, the decrease is likely statistically significant. The YRBSS is a cross-sectional survey of students, so it measures prevalence at each point in time rather than following the same students, and it relies on self-report, which may be affected by recall and social desirability bias; it also misses students who are absent or have left school. Suppose the decline was smaller among students in rural schools, where prevalence remained 23%. I would describe this precisely—prevalence was higher among rural students—without implying that living in a rural area causes vaping, and I would consider social determinants such as advertising exposure, retail density, access to cessation programs and enforcement of age limits. Because the data are cross-sectional, I would not claim that any single policy caused the decline, although it may be associated with flavor restrictions and prevention campaigns. The public health implications are to continue and target prevention and cessation programs, especially in rural schools, enforce sales laws and keep monitoring trends through ongoing surveillance.'},sources:[RULES,YRBS,BRFSS,MMWR]};
